  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mt-8 prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <h2>Biographical headnote</h2>

    <p>
      Dr. Bob Zybach is the Reference Librarian of America&rsquo;s School
      Trust Library. He is a forest and wildfire historian who has spent
      decades researching Pacific Northwest forest history. His research
      also covers wildfire history and Indigenous burning practices.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F1, F7, F8, F9, F24 -->

    <h2>Education</h2>

    <p>
      Zybach holds a Ph.D. in Environmental Sciences from Oregon State
      University (2003). His dissertation is titled <em>The Great Fires:
      Indian Burning and Catastrophic Forest Fire Patterns of the Oregon
      Coast Range, 1491&ndash;1951</em>.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F2, F3, F4 -->

    <p>
      It examines how Native American burning before European contact
      related to later large forest fires in the Oregon Coast Range.
      The Oregon Websites and Watersheds Project (ORWW) uses this
      dissertation as the basis for its work on western Oregon fire
      history.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F5, F19 -->

    <h2>Research and publications</h2>

    <p>
      He co-authored the two-volume <em>Coquelle Trails</em> project
      report with Coquille Chief Don Ivy. The project&rsquo;s website
      launched in 2013. He also co-wrote a history of the Coos Bay Wagon
      Road Lands with George Wasson for the Coquille Indian Tribe.
      That report has not been publicly released.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F16, F17 -->

    <p>
      In 2020, he wrote an article about ORWW&rsquo;s Osbornes fire-lookout
      photo project in <em>Oregon Fish &amp; Wildlife Journal</em>.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F18 -->

    <p>
      Zybach accepted the 2009 Oregon Heritage Excellence Award on behalf
      of ORWW and the Confederated Tribes of Grand Ronde. The Oregon
      Heritage Commission gave the award for the 2008 Owl Ridge Trails
      report.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F15 -->

    <h2>ORWW</h2>

    <p>
      Zybach maintains the ORWW archive, which keeps Jerry Phillips&rsquo;s
      Elliott book online and free. ORWW helps Oregon students, teachers,
      residents and resource managers document and understand the
      region&rsquo;s forest landscape.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F11, F13 -->

    <h2>Reference Librarian of the Library</h2>

    <p>
      He built Parcel Chronicle with Dave Sullivan. Its research desk
      follows his published research method. The project is planned to
      become the Library&rsquo;s Land History Room, curated by Zybach as
      reference librarian.
    </p>
    <!-- facts: F22, F23 -->

    <h2>Where these facts come from</h2>

    <p>
      Education and dissertation details come from Oregon State
      University. ORWW documents the reports, article and award. The
      Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands profile describes his
      research and archive work. Parcel Chronicle describes the project
      and plans for the Land History Room. Dave Sullivan supplied the
      Library appointment information.
    </p>
